[REQ-ISO26262-EDGE-CASE-029]
UID: ISO26262-EDGE-CASE-029
STATUS: Draft
TITLE: Register Allocation Pressure and Spill Decision Non-Determinism

DESCRIPTION:
Register allocation decisions may be non-deterministic or may cause excessive spilling under register pressure, violating deterministic execution timing requirements and WCET guarantees.

CODE_LOCATION:
llvm/lib/Target/PowerPC/PPCRegisterInfo.cpp:80-121
llvm/lib/Target/PowerPC/PPCInstrInfo.cpp:1095-1118
llvm/lib/CodeGen/PrologEpilogInserter.cpp:404-458

ISO26262_REQUIREMENT:
ASIL D - Part 6, Table 35: Deterministic register allocation required for reproducible code generation and WCET analysis.

FAILURE_SCENARIO:
1. Register allocation differs between compiler runs, breaking WCET assumptions
2. Register pressure causes non-deterministic spill decisions
3. Excessive spilling breaks timing guarantees required for safety-critical code
4. Register allocation order affects code generation, violating determinism
5. Spill slot allocation not deterministic, causing different stack layouts

CURRENT_CODE_ANALYSIS:
PPCRegisterInfo.cpp defines register allocation at lines 80-121. PPCInstrInfo.cpp handles spill operations at lines 1095-1118. PrologEpilogInserter.cpp allocates spill slots at lines 404-458. Register allocation may use heuristics that are not fully deterministic, and spill slot allocation may vary between compilation runs.

RECOMMENDATION:
1. Make register allocation deterministic (same input → same output)
2. Ensure spill slot allocation is deterministic
3. Limit register spilling in safety-critical functions
4. Document register allocation requirements for WCET analysis
5. Add compile-time verification of register allocation determinism
6. Verify spill decisions don't break timing guarantees
7. Test register allocation reproducibility

TEST_GAP:
Missing tests for:
- Register allocation determinism verification
- Spill decision reproducibility
- Register pressure handling
- Spill slot allocation determinism
- WCET impact of register spilling

[REQ-ISO26262-EDGE-CASE-030]
UID: ISO26262-EDGE-CASE-030
STATUS: Draft
TITLE: Compiler Intrinsic Safety and Side Effect Handling

DESCRIPTION:
Compiler intrinsics (e.g., __builtin functions, LLVM intrinsics) may have side effects or timing characteristics that are not properly documented or modeled, causing incorrect behavior in safety-critical code or WCET violations.

CODE_LOCATION:
llvm/lib/Target/PowerPC/PPCISelLowering.cpp (intrinsic lowering)
llvm/lib/Target/PowerPC/PPCInstrInfo.td (intrinsic patterns)

ISO26262_REQUIREMENT:
ASIL C/D - Part 6, Table 36: Correct compiler intrinsic behavior required for safety-critical code correctness and timing analysis.

FAILURE_SCENARIO:
1. Compiler intrinsic has undocumented side effects, corrupting system state
2. Intrinsic timing not modeled correctly, breaking WCET analysis
3. Intrinsic generates code with non-deterministic behavior
4. Intrinsic safety properties not verified, causing security vulnerabilities
5. Intrinsic usage violates safety-critical coding standards

CURRENT_CODE_ANALYSIS:
PPCISelLowering.cpp handles intrinsic lowering, but intrinsic behavior, side effects, and timing characteristics may not be fully documented. PPCInstrInfo.td defines patterns for intrinsics but may not capture all safety properties. Intrinsics used in safety-critical code must have verified behavior and documented timing.

RECOMMENDATION:
1. Document all compiler intrinsic side effects and timing characteristics
2. Verify intrinsic behavior matches documentation
3. Model intrinsic timing in WCET analysis
4. Add safety verification for intrinsics used in critical code
5. Document intrinsic usage restrictions for safety-critical systems
6. Add compile-time checks for unsafe intrinsic usage
7. Test intrinsic behavior in all safety-critical scenarios

TEST_GAP:
Missing tests for:
- Intrinsic side effect verification
- Intrinsic timing modeling
- Intrinsic safety property verification
- Intrinsic behavior documentation
- Unsafe intrinsic usage detection

